  "text_md": "with the contours Γ±. It is found that, when grade restricting the branes of Eq. (4.7) to a specific window, the open Witten index was consistent when calculated on the positive\n\nphase and on the negative phase, i.e. χ+k,N = χ−k,N, suggesting strongly that successful brane transport has taken place. The results of a few examples are presented below.\n\nFor KGr(2,4), the basis of branes used was {(0,0),(1,0),(1,1),(2,0),(2,1),(2,2)} and the open Witten indices was calculated to be\n\n\n\n\n\n0 4 6 10 20 20 −4 0 4 4 16 20 −6 −4 0 0 4 6 −10 −4 0 0 4 10 −20 −16 −4 −4 0 4 −20 −20 −6 −10 −4 0\n\nχ+2,4 = χ−2,4 =\n\n. (4.14)\n\n \n\n \n\nA special note is needed here: this was found to be consistent for a set of branes all grade restricted to one of the charge window types in Fig. 4. On the other hand, if a set of branes grade restricted to different window types were mixed together, so that the entire set was not grade restricted to the same charge window type, then the open Witten indices was found to be inconsistent. Thus it is believed that branes must be grade restricted in exactly the same way when computing the Witten index, despite multiple ways of charge restriction being valid for a single choice of theta-angle.\n\nFor KGr(2,5), the basis of branes used was\n\n- {(0,0),(1,0),(1,1),(2,0),(2,1),(2,2), (3,0),(3,1),(3,2),(3,3)}. Then we found14\n\nχ+2,5 = χ−2,5 =\n\n\n\n \n\n0 −5 −10 −15 −40 −50 −35 −105 −175 −175 5 0 −5 −5 −25 −40 −15 −75 −155 −175\n\n10 5 0 0 −5 −10 0 −15 −40 −50 15 5 0 0 −5 −15 −5 −25 −75 −105 40 25 5 5 0 −5 0 −5 −25 −40 50 40 10 15 5 0 0 0 −5 −10 35 15 0 5 0 0 0 −5 −15 −35\n\n105 75 15 25 5 0 5 0 −5 −15 175 155 40 75 25 5 15 5 0 −5 175 175 50 105 40 10 35 15 5 0\n\n\n\n \n\n. (4.15)\n\nFor KGr(2,6), we have 15 generators so, we just present the computation for the subset\n\n- {(0,0),(1,0),(1,1),(2,1),(2,2),(3,2), (3,3),(4,3),(4,4)}:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n0 6 15 70 105 420 490 1764 1764 −6 0 6 36 70 315 420 1624 1764\n\n−15 −6 0 6 15 70 105 420 490 −70 −36 −6 0 6 36 70 315 420\n\nχ+2,6 = χ−2,6 =\n\n−105 −70 −15 −6 0 6 15 70 105 −420 −315 −70 −36 −6 0 6 36 70 −490 −420 −105 −70 −15 −6 0 6 15\n\n. (4.16)\n\n \n\n \n\n−1764 −1624 −420 −315 −70 −36 −6 0 6 −1764 −1764 −490 −420 −105 −70 −15 −6 0\n\n14We remark that (4.15) was computed in [15]. It corresponds to the open Witten index computed in what is called the ’S-basis’ in [15]. More precisely, using the notation of [15]: (χ−−T − χ−−1) = χ+2,5 = χ−2,5.\n\n# 24\n\n",
